WEBVTT

00:00:00.688 --> 00:00:03.535
Tutorial five. The StandardEyes figures.

00:00:07.879 --> 00:00:14.488
The standard refractive surgery figures are drawn from your data, with every group on each panel, in its own colour.

00:00:14.761 --> 00:00:23.784
For stability over time, map one row for each follow-up visit. The spherical equivalent is read from a column, or computed from sphere and cylinder.

00:00:24.091 --> 00:00:28.006
Choose the panel set: full, refractive, or cataract.

00:00:28.415 --> 00:00:34.188
The app tells you exactly which panels you will get, and the list updates as you change the options.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:44.433
Astigmatism follows the JCRS standard: double-angle plots with the centroid, a ninety-five percent region, and one test that compares the centroids.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:50.901
The P values on the figures are clustered by patient, and each figure prints the test that produced its numbers.

00:00:51.173 --> 00:00:57.550
Map the refraction and acuity columns once. The figures and the export tabs all read the same roles.

00:00:57.794 --> 00:01:04.263
Optional workbook tabs add a publication-style table, and tabs laid out for mEYEstro and AstigMATIC.

00:01:05.107 --> 00:01:10.508
This is what you get. The combined figure shows every panel, with every group on it.

00:01:11.688 --> 00:01:19.597
The double-angle plot shows each group's centroid and ninety-five percent region, with the centroid comparison printed on the figure.

00:01:20.772 --> 00:01:27.938
And stability over time shows the mean spherical equivalent at each visit, with the number of eyes behind every point.

00:01:28.696 --> 00:01:33.168
StandardEyes. For ophthalmologists, by ophthalmologists.

